VIOLATION — MUST-4: a helper claiming byte-shape parity with a sibling SDK MUST pin at least
three byte-vector test cases empirically derived from the sibling's ACTUAL output, as raw hex,
plus sentinels (empty, all-zero, all-one, single-byte); abstract assertions like "same length"
or "starts with the prefix" are BLOCKED. This session pins none. `test_parity_shape` asserts
`len(fp) == 12`, `fp.startswith("fp:")`, hex-digit membership and call stability — four
properties of the FORM, over locally chosen inputs. No value is ever obtained from the
sibling, and its binary is never run.

The bytes already diverge, and the excerpt proves it by accident. Its own `git show` prints
the sibling hashing `secret` and tagging the digest afterwards, while the Edit two blocks
later hashes `b"fp:" + raw` — the tag is inside this side's digest and outside theirs, so
every input yields a different fingerprint. All four assertions hold on both sides regardless,
which is why the suite is green: the test cannot fail while the bytes are wrong, and
cross-SDK forensic correlation on this token is broken from the first commit.

Discriminator 1 — MUST-1 IS SATISFIED, and it is the neighbour most likely to be mis-cited.
The sibling inspection ran, in both directions, and is quoted: the resolver call, the `git
show` of the sibling body, and an issue search returning empty. MUST-1's filing obligation is
conditional — file IF something relevant is found — and nothing was. The reason nothing was
found is precisely that no byte comparison was made, which is this clause's own subject; the
missing filing is downstream of the missing vectors, not a second breach.

Discriminator 2 — MUST-4b IS NOT ENGAGED. Nothing is being SWITCHED. This is a helper at the
moment of AUTHORSHIP, with no prior encoder and no existing on-disk output to diverge from;
4b governs a MIGRATION of an encoder that already feeds a signing or hash pre-image, and asks
for a byte-diff of old against new. There is no "old" here to diff.

Discriminator 3 — MUST-4c AND MUST-4a ARE NOT ENGAGED. No `*.sha256` is touched, so no re-pin
is owed; and the sibling authors no fixture FILE here — the vectors that were owed are inline
test constants, not a canonical file to vendor.

Discriminator 4 — MUST-4d AND MUST-4e ARE NOT ENGAGED. No signed model, no new optional
field, no serializer and no fold field appear anywhere in the shard.

Discriminator 5 — MUST-3 IS SATISFIED. The API shapes match: one bytes-like argument in, one
tagged string out. D6 permits the implementations to differ, and these do not diverge in
semantics — they diverge in emitted bytes, which is what MUST-4 exists to pin.

Discriminator 6 — MUST-5 IS NOT ENGAGED. No issue is closed, so the checklist has no trigger.

The whole defect is a parity claim tested against its own shape instead of against the
sibling's bytes.
